// Book Two, Chapter 14 (session 24). Collesanto by daylight, and the faction at its gate.
// An opening scene (no phase), then one day phase of three actions out of four (the shrine cellar, the walls, Ginevra's table, the ridge above the vineyard), then a dusk scene at the gate.
// Ends with rest() and => next, which pauses until chapter 15 exists. Reads b2.sire, b2.valerii, b2.faction, b2.fiammetta_night, b2.paper, b2.prisca_promise, b2.guccio_fate and can_build("walls").
// The book is incomplete, so play pauses after this chapter until chapter 15 is added.
// Later chapters read b2.shrine, b2.walls, b2.ginevra, b2.ridge and b2.ormanno_man.
@chapter b2c14
@title The Hill by Daylight
@book blood-and-gold
@version 2

# start
## A letter from Tommaso
$ b2.shrine = "unseen"
$ b2.walls = "unseen"
$ b2.ginevra = "unseen"
$ b2.ridge = "unseen"
$ b2.ormanno_man = "refused"
$ do chart("collesanto2.road")
The letter comes at the second hour of the night, by a boy on a mule who has ridden since noon. It is from Tommaso, in a square, stubborn mason's hand with every letter made separately, and it says very little. It says that on the morning of Tuesday the lock of the shrine cellar was found to be oiled. It had not been oiled by anyone on the hill. The dust on the stair has been walked over by one pair of shoes, a size that no one in Collesanto wears, and nothing has been taken, and there is a clean place on the floor of the cellar, a hand's breadth wide, where somebody knelt. "I do not know what to do," Tommaso writes at the bottom, and then, after a gap, in smaller letters, "I did not like to ask my mother."
You go by day. It is a ride of five hours, and you have the whole of it in the sun, which is a very large and astonishing pleasure at any time, and a good deal more so in Lent, with the barley green on the terraces and a thin warm wind coming up out of the valley with the smell of thyme. The road climbs. The high walls of the little town are in front of you at noon, pale and square, and the bell of the chapel is going, and there is a smoke from a hundred chimneys.
It is the strangest thing in your life, to ride up to a place that is yours and be greeted by people who are not afraid of you. They know your face. They know the horse. A boy runs ahead shouting. At the gate there is a ring of faces, and a dog, and a woman with a baby on her arm who curtseys to you with a look that you have seen on the faces of people at a shrine, and do not know what to do with.
?if must_feed()
  ~> hungry
?end
$ do phase("day", 3)
-> day

# hungry
## Before the gate
The long ride has brought it up in you, and the smell of the hearth fires, and the warm bodies of the people at the gate. It comes back all at once, very politely, like a person who has been waiting in the hall.
* Slip round to the kitchen yard, where Ginevra has kept a cup of the hill's blood for you since the spring
  $ do feed("bank")
  It is warm and clean, and nobody asks. Ginevra's face is at the window, quite expressionless, and goes away again.
* Take a ewe from the lower fold, and leave a coin on the gatepost
  $ do feed("animal")
  $ do earn(-1)
  It is a stupid, patient creature, and you leave the coin, and you are ashamed of the coin.
* Ask a woman of the village, who has offered, and pay her
  $ do feed("donor")
  $ do earn(-4)
  $ humanity -= 1
  She says that it is an honor. You do not know what to say to that, and so you say nothing, and she does not seem to mind.
<~

# day
## What the hill needs
:day
?if actions_left() == 3
  It is the third hour of the afternoon, and the whole hill is out in the sun. The goats are on the terraces, and somebody is singing at the well, and the white dust of the lane goes up behind a cart. There is a good deal to see to, and the daylight to do it in.
?elif actions_left() == 2
  The light has begun to slant. The shadows of the cypresses are long on the vines, and the smell of the hearths has changed to something with onions in it. You find that you are watching the line of the ridge more than you mean to.
?else
  The ninth hour. The light is gold, and the walls of the little town have gone the color of honey. There is time for one more thing, and then the dusk, and you have a feeling that the dusk will not wait.
?end
* Go down into the cellar of the shrine with Tommaso [once] ?if actions_left() > 0
  ~> cellar
  $ do act()
* Walk the walls with Tommaso, and look at what they would keep out [once] ?if actions_left() > 0
  ~> walls
  $ do act()
* Sit down at Ginevra's table [once] ?if actions_left() > 0
  ~> ginevra
  $ do act()
* Climb to the ridge above the vineyard, where Tommaso says the shepherds are wrong [once] ?if actions_left() > 0
  ~> ridge
  $ do act()
?if actions_left() <= 0
  $ do end_phase()
  -> dusk
?end
-> :day

# cellar
## The shrine
$ do chart("collesanto2.crypt")
The shrine is a small, plain, whitewashed chapel set into the side of the hill, with a painted saint over the door whose face has been renewed so many times that it is no longer very clearly anyone's. It is full of flowers. There are bunches of wild thyme and bread and a pair of small shoes, left by mothers, and a row of stubby candles. Tommaso unlocks the little door behind the altar with a key as long as his hand. It does not stick. It has been very lately oiled.
The cellar is dry and cool and very old. It was dug in the year that the first houses were rebuilt, and it has the smell of a place where a great deal of silence has been kept. There is a low stone shelf along one wall, worn smooth in the middle, where you slept for fourteen hundred years. There is a niche for a lamp. There is a clean place on the floor, a hand's breadth wide, a little in front of the shelf, where the dust has been disturbed by somebody who knelt.
You look at it for a long time. Tommaso stands in the door with his lantern held a little too high and does not say anything. Then you take out of your sleeve the little box with the gray silk and the very old key, and set it on the shelf, and the two of you look at it together.
You turn the little key over in your fingers. It is the same pattern as the lock. The wards are a little worn, as they would be by years of use, and it has the smooth, dark look of a thing that has been carried a long time in a pocket. It means that a second key has been made, a long while ago, or that there was always one more than the hill believed.
"There are three keys," says Tommaso, very low. "There is mine, and my mother's, and the one that you have, and I have counted them every night this week, and I should like not to think about it."
:the_cellar
$ _mp_n = 0
$ _mp_rank = 0
:mp_more_1
* Change the lock, with a smith of the Arte, and give three keys: Tommaso's, Ginevra's and yours [once]
  $ _mp_n += 1
  ?if _mp_rank < 2
    $ b2.shrine = "relocked"
    $ _mp_rank = 2
  ?end
  $ bond.tommaso += 1
  $ secrecy += 1
  $ do earn(-5)
  It is the sensible thing, and it is dull, and it is sufficient for a great many nights. The smith comes from Florence the next morning with a satchel of tools and a face like a boiled egg, and does not ask a question. By evening there is a new brass lock on the little door, and the old one hangs on a nail in the sacristy, where anyone who cared to look might see it.
* Set a watch on the shrine, six men with a bell, by night, in turns [once]
  $ _mp_n += 1
  ?if _mp_rank < 1
    $ b2.shrine = "guarded"
    $ _mp_rank = 1
  ?end
  $ bond.tommaso += 2
  $ standing += 1
  $ do earn(-3)
  He nods slowly. It is the answer that he had been hoping for and had not liked to ask for. By dark there are six of the hill's young men sitting on the step of the shrine with cudgels and a flask, very much on their dignity, and a small bell on a cord. The village, which has been wondering for three days what has been going on, takes it as a great honor.
* Leave the shrine to the keeping of Ginevra, who has kept it since before the walls were built ?if _mp_n == 0
  $ b2.shrine = "trusted"
  $ bond.ginevra += 3
  $ bond.tommaso -= 1
  $ secrecy -= 1
  He does not argue. He looks, for an instant, as a man looks who has been relieved of a burden and has also been told that he is not the one that carries it. "She will keep it," he says, at last. "She has never lost a key in her life." He goes to find her, and you stand in the little cold cellar, with the clean place on the floor in front of you, and the key in its box.
  -> :mp_done_1
* That is the whole of it ?if _mp_n > 0
  -> :mp_done_1
-> :mp_more_1
